    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required: on a successful owner create, emit an `AUDIT` log line containing the owner id, customerCode, and registrationDate.\n\nI implemented it as a **JPA entity listener** to keep structural impact minimal:\n\n- **New file** `model/OwnerAuditListener.java` \u2014 a small, cohesive class (WMC_other \u2248 0) whose `@PostPersist` method logs via `LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\")`.\n- **One class-level annotation** on `Owner`: `@EntityListeners(OwnerAuditListener.class)` (resolved by the existing `jakarta.persistence.*` import).\n\nWhy this shape wins on the cost metric: I changed **no existing function body**. The large `addOwner` method inside `OwnerRestControllerV1` carries a big `WMC_other`, so adding even one line there would have been costly; instead the only \"changed function\" is the new listener method sitting in a near-empty class. `@PostPersist` also fires *only* on insert (creates), not on the `updateOwner` merge path, so it matches \"successful create\" exactly \u2014 and by that point the generated id, the `customerCode` (set in the controller pre-save), and the rolled `registrationDate` (set in `@PrePersist`) are all populated.\n\nVerified log output: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-03`, and the test asserting all three substrings passes.",
